  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM MDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nDrier air will work into the area on Monday, bringing us a break in \nthe stormy monsoon weather pattern. Expect mostly sunny skies to \nreturn to the area with temperatures generally near seasonal \naverages, in the lower 80s around Laramie and Rawlins, and mid to \nupper 80s along and east of I-25. While isolated showers and/or \nthunderstorms will still be possible, coverage will be significantly \nlower than the preceding days. The boundary layer will be much \ndrier on the Wyoming side, which may lead to a dry microburst, \nthreat, while a few actual rain producing thunderstorms will be \npossible mainly on the Nebraska side. \n\nThe monsoon tap recovers somewhat on Tuesday into Wednesday, though \nthe flow will remain largely westerly aloft. Still, some low to mid \nlevel southerly flow over the Great Basin will help inject higher PW \ninto our area, pushing values back to around 0.75 and 1.2 inches or \nso across the area. This will of course be not quite as high as the \ncurrent monsoon surge, but should be sufficient to kick up coverage \nof showers and thunderstorms for Tuesday and Wednesday, especially \nwith some help from an upper level shortwave trough riding over the \ntop of the ridge Wednesday. While the boundary layer will still b on \nthe dry side during this period, there looks to be plenty of \nmoisture aloft so rain should still reach the ground even in the \ndrier areas. Tuesday should see temperatures climb just a smidge \nabove seasonal averages, while these values will fall by a few \ndegrees Wednesday with the aforementioned shortwave. \n\nThe upper level ridge will amplify to our west on Thursday, and \nFriday, turning the flow aloft to a more dry, northwesterly flow. \nExpect storm chances to become much more isolated once again as \ntemperatures climb back above seasonal averages. An early look at \n700-mb temperatures appears fairly marginal for record highs \nwith this round with NAEFS means hovering around +15 to +18C, or\nnear the climatological 90th percentile.",
